meta {
  name: 06 - Chat Agent (Gemma Cloud - Blocked On This Network)
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/runs/wait
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "assistant_id": "agent",
    "input": {
      "changeme": "Say hello in one word"
    }
  }
}

vars:pre-request {
  baseUrl: http://localhost:2024
}

docs {
  The "agent" graph (src/agent/graph.py) sends the input to Ollama's
  gemma4:31b-cloud model. On this machine, outbound TLS to ollama.com fails
  (corporate network - confirmed with curl -v: SEC_E_ILLEGAL_MESSAGE during
  the TLS handshake), so cloud models can't be reached.
  
  Verified response on this network (2026-09-16), HTTP 200 but with a
  run-level error payload instead of a real reply:
  
    {
      "__error__": {
        "error": "ResponseError",
        "message": "An internal error occurred"
      }
    }
  
  Good example of why testing via a raw API client matters: LangGraph
  Studio's chat UI would just show a generic failure, but here you see the
  exact shape LangGraph Server uses to report a node exception (top-level
  "__error__" key) rather than an HTTP 4xx/5xx.
  
  This will start working once either:
    - the network/proxy allows TLS to ollama.com, or
    - you point `context.model` / OLLAMA_MODEL at a model that's already
      pulled locally (e.g. "llama3:latest"), which doesn't need ollama.com.
}
